{"kind":"artifact","text":"docs at a86f35e13ef871813338a840a5006eb8c1fe0519: the shipped sequencer queue bound, documented.\n\ndocs/reference/limits.md gains a Concurrent submissions section stating the bound of 32, that the count includes the submission holding the sequencer lock so 32 means one in progress and 31 waiting, and that over the bound a submission is refused rather than queued with `sequencer at capacity`.\n\nThe ordering is the part worth documenting, and the page says it plainly: the refusal is taken before the signed intent is parsed, before any admission hook runs, before the payload tree is written, and before anything is chained onto the sequence ref. That is what makes it a back-pressure signal a caller can act on rather than a late failure after the cost is already paid, and it is why a caller can tell it apart from a malformed or unauthorized submission programmatically.\n\nThe page also says the bound belongs to Gitseq's resident, and that a program embedding the kernel directly may leave it unset for an unbounded queue — the embedding opt-out, not a posture Gitseq takes. Without that sentence a reader would reasonably conclude the kernel is bounded by default, which is exactly the confusion that let an earlier head ship an option nobody set.\n\nFront matter cites the two behaviour artifacts this page describes, 9ee4f7cd at internal/kernel and 6b5e0b34 at internal/app, so the page flares when either moves. Those are the replacement artifacts on the clean lineage, not the stale candidates they superseded; citing the stale ones would have made this page stale from birth for the same reason they were.\n\nThis rests on its authorizing request, my promise, and 8c9b7432, the live artifact for what main carries at docs.","body":{"commit":"a86f35e13ef871813338a840a5006eb8c1fe0519","path":"docs"}}